WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.930
[MUSIC].

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:07.951
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:07.951 --> 00:00:09.248
Please if you can go for the far seat.

00:00:09.248 --> 00:00:16.608
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; It's an absolute honor

00:00:16.608 --> 00:00:20.122
to have you here, and if you don't mind we
want to take you back in time a little bit

00:00:20.122 --> 00:00:24.125
and ask about your time or your thinking
straight out of business school.

00:00:24.125 --> 00:00:26.745
So we were very lucky to get
enrolled into this program and

00:00:26.745 --> 00:00:29.065
we got two years to add
value to ourselves.

00:00:29.065 --> 00:00:33.125
But most importantly we
are encouraged to take time off to

00:00:33.125 --> 00:00:37.000
reflect on our career choices and what
it is that we want to do after school.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.380
Naturally we are very,
very confused at this point.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:40.920
Take that advice to heart.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.930
So how intentional were you about your
career coming out of business school?

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:48.790
&gt;&gt; I would tell you, I was sort of
an accidental investment banker and

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:49.620
an accidental CEO.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:57.790
And a matter of fact,
when I was recruiting at Goldman Sachs,

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.640
I was always very suspicious
of students who said they

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:05.750
came out of their mother's womb knowing
they wanted to be an investment banker.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:10.840
So to step back,
you won't hear many stories like mine,

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.100
because I was an English
major at Dartmouth.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.560
I went there thinking I wanted
to be a forest ranger or

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.390
work somehow in conservation.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:22.070
I was going to go to Oxford.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.230
I had a rental scholarship.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.350
I was going to study English.

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.670
But I had a very low draft number.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.650
And I could get into the ROTC at Harvard.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.220
And so I went to Harvard Business School.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:39.870
I just turned 22,
I hardly knew what business was.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:43.350
In those days, if you were a good
student in an Ivy League School,

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.292
you didn't admit you wanted
to go into business.

00:01:45.292 --> 00:01:50.200
So when I told some of my friends I was
going to business school they almost

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.938
throw up.

00:01:52.938 --> 00:01:58.036
So I went with no business experience,

00:01:58.036 --> 00:02:05.455
and I got to Harvard and
I'd been a very serious student.

00:02:05.455 --> 00:02:10.590
And I worked hard in college, but
everybody seemed so much older.

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.640
You know, they were 27 or 28.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.370
Some of them were married,
and I was dating my wife,

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.660
Wendy, who was at Wellesley.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.516
So I was out at Wellesley really
more than I was at business school.

00:02:22.516 --> 00:02:27.852
And so
I didn't even know what an investment

00:02:27.852 --> 00:02:33.186
banker was, and I certainly wouldn't have

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:38.246
said I wanted to be an investment banker.

00:02:38.246 --> 00:02:42.115
I did a lot of work in one of
the professor's classes, and

00:02:42.115 --> 00:02:44.220
he had worked in the Pentagon.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.630
And so there is this small group,

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:54.120
this analysis group that McNamara
had started when he was there.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.820
And I got asked one day whether I
want to interview for this group.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:06.390
And since I was going to
go in the Navy anyway,

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.700
then this meant I wasn't going to
have to go on my Navy cruise and

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:15.530
I could be with my wife to be and so on.

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:17.410
It seemed like a pretty good deal.

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:23.170
And it turns out that this group,
there were six people,

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.090
four of us went on to become CEOs.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:32.660
One was Steve Hadley who became the
national security advisor to George Bush.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:36.140
Another was John Sprat who was
a couple years older who became

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:38.680
Chairman of
the House Financial Services Committee.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.560
So it was an interesting
group to work with..

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:47.040
So that's how my career took shape.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.730
&gt;&gt; So
you actually came out of business school,

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:52.880
you took the two public office positions,
Department of Defense and

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:55.900
then followed by
the President Nixon's administration.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.040
&gt;From the outside it looked like
you had it all figured out and

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:00.990
you were well on the way to
getting a Senate seat [LAUGH].

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:08.940
&gt;&gt; No, I did not have it all figured out,
and I never worked in business.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:12.750
So I wore a suit on special
occasions to go to church.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.410
I never worked in business.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:22.640
And so I got there to this analysis group,
and I learned a lot when I was there.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:29.410
It's amazing how much I
learned because I have this

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:34.496
view that it's not just
coming up with a right idea.

00:04:34.496 --> 00:04:40.740
Everybody can learn the computational
skills, analytical skills and so on.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:42.600
But it's the people skills that matter.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.270
&gt;&gt; So why the shift into Goldman Sachs?

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:46.610
&gt;&gt; Well, I would say this.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:49.384
You're moving faster than I.

00:04:49.384 --> 00:04:53.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because literally, what I learned when

00:04:53.440 --> 00:05:00.010
I was there was I didn't know
what the word mentor was.

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:03.390
But I found that when I would work for
five or

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:09.030
six different people and on different
assignments, so I did the same work.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.870
And some really appreciated my skills and
others didn't.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:16.280
Some gave me the help I needed,
others didn't.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:19.810
And one of the things I worked on,
I worked on my first bail-out there,

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:20.890
believe it or not.

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:25.250
It was the Lockheed failure and
the Lockheed loan guarantee.

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:32.380
So I had a chance to do that, and I got
to see how the political process worked.

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:37.340
And as a result of that
John Connolly had introduced

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:41.830
me to some people in the White House
who wanted me to go there.

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:46.258
And then I learned

00:05:46.258 --> 00:05:51.280
that almost as important as what I was
doing was who I was doing it with.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:52.440
It really made a big difference.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:57.280
So I interviewed all of the six different
directors of the Domestic Council, and

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:00.920
I picked the guy that maybe
the least interesting work

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:02.370
was going to be under him.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.270
But I thought I would
learn a lot from him.

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:09.560
And then it turned out I got to work
on something I was very interested in,

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:13.550
minimum wage, tax policy,
I was the liaison for Treasury.

00:06:14.750 --> 00:06:20.420
And so one of the things I
learned was that it was fun for

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:23.216
me to work on financial problems.

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:31.350
And that's why I decided
on investment banking.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:36.087
And even investment banking

00:06:36.087 --> 00:06:41.218
was accidental because I knew I

00:06:41.218 --> 00:06:46.750
didn't want to work in New York.

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:51.432
So I talked to regional investment banks,
and it was only when I had a job

00:06:51.432 --> 00:06:55.490
offer from a company called
William Blair where the CEO said,

00:06:55.490 --> 00:07:00.740
yeah we'd like to have you come here but
you're very aggressive, ambitious.

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:03.660
We don't think you'll be
happy with a regional firm.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.940
Why don't you talk to Goldman Sachs and
Saloman Brothers?

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.300
And with Goldman Sachs,

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:14.180
they rank like number ten in
the league tables in those days.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:18.857
And I ended up talking with a few
other firms, Morgan Stanley,

00:07:18.857 --> 00:07:23.850
White Weld, so on, but I really
liked the people at Goldman Sachs.

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:27.470
I met people like Bob Rubin and
Steve Freedman and

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:30.675
a whole bunch of names you wouldn't know,
but they were.

00:07:30.675 --> 00:07:33.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; People weren't there, but

00:07:33.130 --> 00:07:34.940
they made a big.

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:38.720
So I just basically said,
I feel comfortable with the culture.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:44.275
And I learned that almost as important
as what you do, is who you do it with.

00:07:44.275 --> 00:07:48.031
But if you had asked me out of college,
was I going to be an investment banker,

00:07:48.031 --> 00:07:51.510
I couldn't have told you what
an investment banker was.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:56.044
And in business school when I was
there The hot thing in those days,

00:07:56.044 --> 00:08:00.281
when I graduated from
Harvard Business School, let me say,

00:08:00.281 --> 00:08:05.949
the first two years I was at Goldman
Sachs, they lost money in '73 and '74.

00:08:05.949 --> 00:08:10.651
That people weren't going to investment
banks out of business school,

00:08:10.651 --> 00:08:13.360
they all wanted to go to Mackenzie or BCG.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.348
And the hottest thing was Hilton Head.

00:08:16.348 --> 00:08:19.020
You know, Sea Pines,
real estate development.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:22.115
You pictured people lighting
cigars with hundred dollar bills.

00:08:22.115 --> 00:08:22.637
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:08:22.637 --> 00:08:25.292
so, wherever the students
wanted to go that was all,

00:08:25.292 --> 00:08:28.721
just about before it would blow up,
that's where they wanted to go.

00:08:28.721 --> 00:08:33.539
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, 32 years later, you made the exact

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:40.200
opposite transition going into public
office as the Secretary of Treasury.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.662
So can you walk us through how you made
that decision and how were you a different

00:08:43.662 --> 00:08:46.564
person then than the person who
went into finance 32 years ago?

00:08:46.564 --> 00:08:54.910
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but again, brevity may not be
one of my virtues but will say this.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:59.006
That when you went to Goldman Sachs,
and you went into investment banking,

00:08:59.006 --> 00:09:01.822
you didn't go because you
wanted to run something,

00:09:01.822 --> 00:09:04.004
you wanted to work with clients, okay?

00:09:04.004 --> 00:09:07.220
If you wanted to run something,
you went to work for a real company.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:13.270
So I had no interest in running something,
I wanted to work with clients.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:17.520
And so again I'm just saying
this in terms of career advice

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:21.590
because I am suspicious
of career engineers.

00:09:21.590 --> 00:09:24.510
People that plan it out
every step of the way.

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:27.720
And I'd had people come up to me at
all the time at Goldman Sachs and

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:31.710
say Hank, should I do arbitrage or
should I do

00:09:34.040 --> 00:09:37.740
private equity or
should I be doing corporate finance?

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:42.030
And I'd say, what is it you like,
what is it you're going to do well?

00:09:42.030 --> 00:09:47.860
And so I only wanted to work with clients.

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:52.750
And so
I was in Chicago working with clients and

00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:55.150
was really focused on
a wide range of clients.

00:09:55.150 --> 00:10:01.079
I turned down a variety of promotions,
I stayed in Chicago.

00:10:01.079 --> 00:10:04.725
When finally they put me on
the management committee,

00:10:04.725 --> 00:10:09.745
I ended up starting a private
equity division from Chicago.

00:10:09.745 --> 00:10:14.740
Co-heading investment banking
from Chicago and getting

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:19.610
Asia in my portfolio because my partner
in New York said he wanted Europe and

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:23.127
he said, Asia's closer to Chicago.

00:10:23.127 --> 00:10:26.629
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So that's how, and I did.

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:30.993
So my career took a whole lot of,

00:10:30.993 --> 00:10:36.585
only as time went on that I
learned that I enjoyed managing.

00:10:36.585 --> 00:10:41.716
And then only when we had
Bob Rubin went to run,

00:10:41.716 --> 00:10:49.148
went to work for Bill Clinton in
the White House and then Treasury.

00:10:49.148 --> 00:10:52.550
And his co-head had a physical problem and

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:56.450
had to leave at the same time
Goldman Sachs had trading losses.

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:57.432
And I was in Chicago and

00:10:57.432 --> 00:11:00.238
the management committee had to
pick the next management team.

00:11:00.238 --> 00:11:04.930
I told my wife it's going to happen over
the weekend, don't worry, I'll come back.

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:10.420
And Goldman Sachs had losses and
I had to have her send a couple suits.

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:13.030
I had to go buy stuff, I stayed there.

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:17.160
And I told her, I would just do this for
two years and it was a rolling two years.

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:18.518
I was there for 12 years.

00:11:18.518 --> 00:11:20.580
So I would just,

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:26.120
I tell that story to say that if I think
I had said I want to run Goldman Sachs,

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:30.430
I would have come in and I don't think
I would ever want to run Goldman Sachs.

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:34.130
I just focused on doing what
I was doing in front of me.

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:37.333
Enjoying it, learning and growing.

00:11:37.333 --> 00:11:46.290
Now in terms of, boy, being Secretary
of the Treasury, it was interesting.

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:51.704
I sit in this group because I
think back to 2006 because I

00:11:51.704 --> 00:11:56.793
had agreed to be a speaker at
Harvard Business School and

00:11:56.793 --> 00:12:04.300
I had agreed to do that long before
I thought I'd be Treasury Secretary.

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:07.710
And so
I was standing up talking to students

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:10.320
knowing that I was going to
be Treasury Secretary.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:14.482
And I remember thinking, I use to go and
recruit all the time, and

00:12:14.482 --> 00:12:18.666
I would say to people, you know,
the world is changing quickly.

00:12:18.666 --> 00:12:21.732
The pace of change is increasing,

00:12:21.732 --> 00:12:26.830
embrace change,
change is your friend, etc, etc.

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:29.784
And I was thinking, my stomach was
churning as I was thinking about that

00:12:29.784 --> 00:12:32.960
change, and thinking my God,
I'm going to have to eat my own cooking.

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:36.655
Because we all say we like change,
but we hate change, really.

00:12:36.655 --> 00:12:38.995
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's a difficult thing.

00:12:38.995 --> 00:12:46.290
And so as I was running Goldman Sachs,
I enjoyed it.

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:51.661
I thought I was doing it well and
so when I got the first approach

00:12:51.661 --> 00:12:56.380
from the White House,
I immediately turned it down.

00:12:56.380 --> 00:13:01.256
I had a wife and a mother and two kids who

00:13:01.256 --> 00:13:06.298
were very, did not favor George Bush.

00:13:06.298 --> 00:13:11.621
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:13:11.621 --> 00:13:15.247
&gt;&gt; And so I turned it down and

00:13:15.247 --> 00:13:20.270
then they came back a second time.

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:26.490
This was when I did a lot
of work with China, and

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:29.630
so Hu Jintao,
who was the President of China,

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:33.820
was going to be at the White House
sometime in the spring of 2006.

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:36.650
And I was asked, they didn't want to
give the Chinese a state dinner so

00:13:36.650 --> 00:13:39.710
they gave them a state lunch
if you can believe that.

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:44.180
So I was asked for the state lunch and
I was asked to have dinner or

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:47.180
meet with George Bush in
the residence beforehand.

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.450
When I do that and I'd agreed to do it and

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:53.020
then as the time got closer I said I can't
do that, I can't turn down the president.

00:13:53.020 --> 00:13:55.710
I'm not going to leave Goldman Sachs and
join the administration.

00:13:55.710 --> 00:13:57.304
So I blew it off at the last minute.

00:13:57.304 --> 00:14:02.559
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so, I went into the White House for

00:14:02.559 --> 00:14:07.301
the lunch and everybody was sort
of a little bit cool to me.

00:14:07.301 --> 00:14:11.497
And as I was walking out with Wendy
it was a beautiful spring day and

00:14:11.497 --> 00:14:14.168
I was looking at the Treasury Building and

00:14:14.168 --> 00:14:18.210
I thought back to the days when I
was in the White House as a kid and

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:23.200
I'd go over and meet with
George Schultz when he was at Treasury.

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:25.160
And sort of a cloud came over my face and

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:31.500
Wendy said to me, well I hope you
didn't turn it down for me, sweetie,

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:35.530
because if it was really important
to you I would have agreed to do it.

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:37.861
And I said no, no, no.

00:14:37.861 --> 00:14:42.660
And then when they came back the third
time I just realized that it was

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:43.930
fear of failure.

00:14:43.930 --> 00:14:47.820
I realized that I knew I could do
a good job as Treasury Secretary.

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:52.414
I couldn't think of many people that had
gone from business to government and

00:14:52.414 --> 00:14:56.381
come back, left with a higher
reputation than they'd come with.

00:14:56.381 --> 00:14:59.715
And so as soon as I realized that,
I said, we'll,

00:14:59.715 --> 00:15:02.700
I'm not going to give into that and
I accepted.

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:05.320
And, my family was in shock for awhile.

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:09.530
My mother said to me, by gosh,
you started off working for Nixon and

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:11.590
you're going to now finish working for
Bush.

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:13.145
You should be ashamed of yourself.

00:15:13.145 --> 00:15:14.078
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, I cried.

00:15:14.078 --> 00:15:17.442
[LAUGH] Etc, etc.

00:15:17.442 --> 00:15:21.292
And they all decided as I did
that they had great respect and

00:15:21.292 --> 00:15:24.180
liked when they saw how he worked with me.

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:27.331
And I had great respect for him,
and it was a real pleasure.

00:15:27.331 --> 00:15:31.190
But so I tell you that in more detail,
do you see how some of these decisions.

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:33.806
It's not like I said now I'm
going to go to Goldman Sachs and

00:15:33.806 --> 00:15:35.620
now be Treasury Secretary.

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:37.910
Things just worked out.

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:40.790
&gt;&gt; But my mother was not a big
fan of George Bush's vote and

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:45.110
this actually makes me feel much better,
and

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:48.940
I'm sure it does all of you, about not
having that strong of a plan coming up.

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:51.930
So hopefully this will work out well.

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:56.380
The next question I want to ask
is You stated several times that

00:15:56.380 --> 00:15:58.870
the work-life balance is
very important to you.

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:01.205
And we are trying one
of the main skills or

00:16:01.205 --> 00:16:05.798
core skills we're trying to gain is the
ability to influence large institution.

00:16:05.798 --> 00:16:10.675
Now the cutthroat environment,
100 hour plus work weeks at Goldman Sachs,

00:16:10.675 --> 00:16:15.408
are to some extent in contradiction
with the work-life balance that you've

00:16:15.408 --> 00:16:17.767
stated several times that you value.

00:16:17.767 --> 00:16:22.106
So when you were rising through the ranks
and eventually running the organization,

00:16:22.106 --> 00:16:25.356
how did you think about managing or
influencing that culture?

00:16:25.356 --> 00:16:30.258
&gt;&gt; I'll tell you another
story because then,

00:16:30.258 --> 00:16:35.300
I think it was 1999 after we went public.

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:39.193
I had a nephew who was graduating
from Kellogg Business School.

00:16:39.193 --> 00:16:43.854
So, I was the speaker there and
my speech was on work-life balance.

00:16:43.854 --> 00:16:48.878
And so, I told the story,
which was absolutely true,

00:16:48.878 --> 00:16:53.576
that I was in the Chicago
office of Goldman Sachs and

00:16:53.576 --> 00:16:59.148
I had a three year old and
a one year old and I was working hard,

00:16:59.148 --> 00:17:06.170
coming home late and my wife Wendy said
to me, Hank, this is unacceptable.

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:11.660
And you either are going to come home and

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:16.780
take an active part in raising the kids or
you're going to come

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:21.492
home some night and the kids are still
going to be here and I'm going to be gone.

00:17:21.492 --> 00:17:25.778
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so, I went to my boss and

00:17:25.778 --> 00:17:30.918
I said I really need to
do something differently.

00:17:30.918 --> 00:17:34.930
I really do and
I was off to a very good start.

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:38.050
And so he said, look it,
you do what you need to do.

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:42.073
So my office was on the 60th
floor of the Sears Tower, and

00:17:42.073 --> 00:17:47.413
if you can believe this, I would run
at 4:30 from there to get the 4:42

00:17:47.413 --> 00:17:51.961
train from the Chicago Northwestern
to Barrington, Illinois.

00:17:51.961 --> 00:17:53.594
It got in at 5:25.

00:17:53.594 --> 00:17:56.117
I was home at quarter of six.

00:17:56.117 --> 00:18:00.747
I would give the kids dinner.

00:18:00.747 --> 00:18:04.312
I'd give them a bath and
I'd read the goodnight story.

00:18:04.312 --> 00:18:08.537
And I'd be reading In the Great Green Room
with the Red Balloon, Goodnight, Moon.

00:18:08.537 --> 00:18:09.287
I'd be going through this.

00:18:09.287 --> 00:18:12.996
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so, I do not read with expression

00:18:12.996 --> 00:18:17.150
and so once Wendy came in,
if you've heard me read a speech,

00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:20.828
you know it's not a very
pleasant thing to listen to.

00:18:20.828 --> 00:18:23.634
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so Wendy came in and she says,

00:18:23.634 --> 00:18:26.740
slow down, read with expression.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:29.820
When I did that the kids both
started crying and said,

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:32.087
no, read like a daddy not like a mommy.

00:18:32.087 --> 00:18:34.555
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:18:34.555 --> 00:18:41.150
I told that story to
the Kellogg Graduating Class.

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:42.310
And just like this,

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:46.460
It was the same way, everybody laughed and
particularly the women, they loved it.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:49.340
And the mothers loved it,
and they all liked me.

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:53.379
And I walked out and
there was a competitive partner at

00:18:53.379 --> 00:18:57.255
Salomon Brothers at the time,
named J Ira Harris.

00:18:57.255 --> 00:18:59.900
And he said, you hypocrite.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:03.232
You're standing up there,
giving that speech and

00:19:03.232 --> 00:19:08.470
then you just work the living daylights
out of the kids at Goldman Sachs.

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:09.499
How can you do this?

00:19:09.499 --> 00:19:11.805
Where does this come from?

00:19:11.805 --> 00:19:16.785
And I said well, I would just tell
you what I say to everybody that

00:19:16.785 --> 00:19:20.095
came to me for advice at Goldman Sachs.

00:19:20.095 --> 00:19:24.405
I said, you can't ask the firm to

00:19:24.405 --> 00:19:26.825
tell you how many hours
a week you're going to work.

00:19:26.825 --> 00:19:33.255
If you're working 50 how about 55,
60 how about 65?

00:19:33.255 --> 00:19:35.080
You have to learn to say no.

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:38.298
You have to plan your own life.

00:19:38.298 --> 00:19:44.046
And that the people spend so
much time on their career and

00:19:44.046 --> 00:19:48.335
so little relative time on their family.

00:19:48.335 --> 00:19:51.783
And you just get out of it,
what you invest in it.

00:19:51.783 --> 00:19:54.170
And you can't be a grunt.

00:19:54.170 --> 00:19:57.190
Think people need to be very careful.

00:19:57.190 --> 00:20:00.650
You need to do things you enjoy and
that you're going to do well.

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:04.490
And if the only way you can do well is
working more hours than someone else,

00:20:04.490 --> 00:20:05.240
you're going to lose out,

00:20:05.240 --> 00:20:08.320
because there's always going to be
someone else who's going to work more.

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:13.842
And so you need to find something and

00:20:13.842 --> 00:20:21.217
you need to work to have
a life away from the job.

00:20:21.217 --> 00:20:24.859
And I can tell you this,
I know everybody has said this to you,

00:20:24.859 --> 00:20:29.343
but I can assure you this is this case,
probably the one thing I've done more

00:20:29.343 --> 00:20:34.340
than anyone else who has ever run
a Wall Street firm is work with clients.

00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:39.140
Because I worked from all the way
up in investment banking, and

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:42.430
when I ran Goldman Sachs,
I still advised CEOs.

00:20:42.430 --> 00:20:45.530
And I handled clients,
because I thought that was important.

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:47.810
I worked with heads of state,
I advised them.

00:20:47.810 --> 00:20:53.320
I advised the president of China,
Germany CEOs.

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:57.620
I've worked with many, many of them that
have been successful professionally.

00:20:57.620 --> 00:21:01.160
I've worked with many, many people that
didn't get where they thought they were

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:04.820
going to get and retired,
and came to me for advice.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:09.400
And I know of many, many people that
didn't quite get to where they wanted to

00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:12.880
get with their career, and they're happy.

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:14.600
They're happy and they're well-balanced.

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:18.359
I don't know anyone that says,
boy, I had a great career and

00:21:18.359 --> 00:21:23.170
I'm happy because I screwed up my life
outside of my career, my family life.

00:21:23.170 --> 00:21:28.440
There's no one that feels that way.

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:31.860
And I take it to the point
where I encourage people to do

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:35.070
things also In the not-for-profit area.

00:21:35.070 --> 00:21:38.720
When I ran Goldman Sachs,
I chaired the Nature Conservancy.

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:40.580
I chaired the Peregrine Fund.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:44.510
I encouraged people to do these things.

00:21:44.510 --> 00:21:46.740
It makes you better at your career.

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:54.600
It's not easy and don't expect
your bosses to take care of it.

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:56.200
I think you need to take care of it.

00:21:56.200 --> 00:22:01.200
You need to get yourself in
a situation where you can balance it.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:05.100
It is really, really important,
really important.

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:09.434
And you know, it's tricky.

00:22:09.434 --> 00:22:11.614
&gt;&gt; We have some class members
who got offers already, so

00:22:11.614 --> 00:22:12.829
I hope they can pull this off.

00:22:12.829 --> 00:22:13.691
Good luck.

00:22:13.691 --> 00:22:18.205
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Moving forward to the point
when you became the Secretary of Treasury,

00:22:18.205 --> 00:22:23.137
you've stated before that you identified
specific risks that the financial system

00:22:23.137 --> 00:22:26.054
was running,
specific financial industries and

00:22:26.054 --> 00:22:31.080
you've actually pointed it out to
President Bush well before the crises.

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:35.550
One of the core skills we're trying to
gain out of our experience here is how to

00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:41.600
think and strategize in a preventive
manner to avoid crises.

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:46.803
So what did you do between the years of
2006 and 2008 to implement that change?

00:22:46.803 --> 00:22:51.237
And for example, would it have been
possible to implement something like

00:22:51.237 --> 00:22:54.312
the Dodd-Frank Act earlier or
maybe in 2006,

00:22:54.312 --> 00:22:57.616
rather than only implement
it after hell broke loose?

00:22:57.616 --> 00:23:02.233
&gt;&gt; In 2006, after I became
Treasury Secretary of the President,

00:23:02.233 --> 00:23:06.129
my first meeting he took
the economic team to Camp David.

00:23:06.129 --> 00:23:10.480
And I was asked to talk about
entitlements, because that was one of

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:15.000
the reasons that I was coming down
was to work on entitlement reform.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:18.481
And I said no,
I'd like to speak about financial crises,

00:23:18.481 --> 00:23:23.470
because I'd been through one in '94 and
'98 and I saw excesses in the system.

00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:27.632
Now, I saw nothing like
the one we had coming.

00:23:27.632 --> 00:23:32.610
And when the President said to me,
what will cause it?

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:35.396
I said, I don't know.

00:23:35.396 --> 00:23:40.523
Afterwards, it'll be clear, people with
20/20 hindsight will say it's obvious and

00:23:40.523 --> 00:23:43.742
there will always be someone
that says I predicted it, but

00:23:43.742 --> 00:23:46.650
that person won't predict the next one.

00:23:46.650 --> 00:23:53.769
Okay, because they're unpredictable
in terms of the timing and the cause.

00:23:53.769 --> 00:23:55.680
&gt;&gt; If you don't mind me interrupting,
we're of the shelf.

00:23:55.680 --> 00:24:00.481
The derivatives and credit default swaps
not apparently toxic at that point.

00:24:00.481 --> 00:24:03.780
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so what I did when I came down.

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:05.460
He said, what might cause it?

00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:07.810
I talked about credit default swaps.

00:24:07.810 --> 00:24:09.950
I talked about a huge problem I saw,

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:14.830
which we were working with Tim Geithner
at the New York Fed to clean up.

00:24:14.830 --> 00:24:17.470
Where there just, I won't go into it, but

00:24:17.470 --> 00:24:21.180
there's a very big technical
problem with credit default swaps.

00:24:21.180 --> 00:24:26.760
I thought the hedge funds, hedge
fund's connectivity to banks, I didn't

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:31.830
look at, say, mortgages, I knew there
were some issues with subprime.

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:35.898
But the idea that the whole
housing marking would drop.

00:24:35.898 --> 00:24:42.903
We'd been looking at, I was born in 1946,
and ever since World War II,

00:24:42.903 --> 00:24:48.817
if you bought a diversified
portfolio of residential mortgages

00:24:48.817 --> 00:24:54.240
the only risk you had was you
got your money back too soon.

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:58.400
Because there was no nationwide
decline of housing prices,

00:24:58.400 --> 00:25:00.390
it was a commercial mortgage market.

00:25:00.390 --> 00:25:04.440
And so we didn't see that,
we didn't see the money market.

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:11.530
But the advantage of having said that was,
the huge advantage to me, was

00:25:11.530 --> 00:25:15.710
I had a year to build the relationships
we needed to build before the crisis.

00:25:15.710 --> 00:25:18.998
We couldn't,
the horse was already out of the barn,

00:25:18.998 --> 00:25:21.420
there were, the excesses were there.

00:25:23.300 --> 00:25:26.680
But I had a year where we had something

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:29.450
called the President's working
group on financial markets.

00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:33.285
So then I started working
with Ben Bernanke and

00:25:33.285 --> 00:25:38.390
Tim Geithner, and
we didn't uncover the problems.

00:25:38.390 --> 00:25:42.211
We never thought about money markets,
or the tri-party repo market, but

00:25:42.211 --> 00:25:45.624
we did a lot of work together, and
we built strong relationships.

00:25:45.624 --> 00:25:47.973
And then most important of all,

00:25:47.973 --> 00:25:52.845
I had a chance to build a relationship
with George Bush, because,

00:25:52.845 --> 00:25:57.369
if there's any point I want to
make to you all, it's all about

00:25:57.369 --> 00:26:03.460
interpersonal relationships,
it's all about working with others.

00:26:03.460 --> 00:26:06.968
Getting anything done that's
important requires that.

00:26:06.968 --> 00:26:10.329
I had negotiated quite a deal
when I came to the white house,

00:26:10.329 --> 00:26:13.973
because I got turned down several
times when I came to Treasury.

00:26:13.973 --> 00:26:18.414
And so that I could make all
the appointments, regardless of political

00:26:18.414 --> 00:26:22.336
party, at treasury,
that I'd be their primary spokesman and

00:26:22.336 --> 00:26:26.864
adviser on domestic and economic issues,
how things would be staffed.

00:26:26.864 --> 00:26:30.819
But I realized when I got there
that no matter what I negotiated,

00:26:30.819 --> 00:26:35.141
if it didn't work between me and
the President the whole thing wasn't

00:26:35.141 --> 00:26:38.308
going to work, and
it would be my fault and not his.

00:26:38.308 --> 00:26:41.340
And I'd been trained as
an investment banker.

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:45.210
I wasn't running a big firm
where people gave orders, I was,

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:46.790
I had to get down on my knees and

00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:51.770
persuade certain partners to stay, most of
them thought they were smarter than I was.

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:55.140
I had learned to work with clients, so

00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:59.500
I could run a firm and advise a principal.

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:02.410
So I knew how to do that, and

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:07.110
so I had an opportunity to build a very
close relationship with the President,

00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:11.250
a relationship of trust before
the crisis hit, and with Congress.

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:13.210
So that was a key, and

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:17.870
that was one of the benefits of getting
ready, but we certainly didn't.

00:27:19.120 --> 00:27:25.010
If I had been omniscient, and
I'd known exactly what was happening,

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:26.950
I couldn't have got
the powers a day earlier.

00:27:26.950 --> 00:27:29.492
Because I started working in Fannie and
Freddie in 2006.

00:27:29.492 --> 00:27:34.755
It just it took a crisis to get
what we needed from Congress.

00:27:34.755 --> 00:27:40.492
&gt;&gt; TARP was labeled as as
the bailout of the guilty rich,

00:27:40.492 --> 00:27:44.830
at the expense of taxpayer money.

00:27:44.830 --> 00:27:48.910
So that was a very tough decision for
you to make, of course.

00:27:48.910 --> 00:27:51.240
But how did you think
about public messaging?

00:27:51.240 --> 00:27:55.220
Did you try to gain support, or
at least understanding of the necessity

00:27:55.220 --> 00:27:58.087
of the decision by the public,
and how did that go?

00:27:58.087 --> 00:28:00.830
&gt;&gt; So it's a very interesting thing.

00:28:00.830 --> 00:28:05.402
When I left Treasury,
there was a poll that showed, and

00:28:05.402 --> 00:28:09.489
I don't remember the numbers
exactly right, but

00:28:09.489 --> 00:28:14.353
something like 90% of the people
were against TARP, and

00:28:14.353 --> 00:28:17.682
60% or 70% were against torture.

00:28:17.682 --> 00:28:22.333
And yet, a big part of
the population was also scared to

00:28:22.333 --> 00:28:26.970
death we were going to
have a financial crisis.

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:31.750
So I was never successful, that was one
of the big failings of letting people

00:28:31.750 --> 00:28:35.740
know what we did was for
the American people.

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:39.650
It wasn't for Wall Street, and

00:28:41.450 --> 00:28:44.930
I think most people to this day still
don't know that all the money we

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:51.183
put in that, when I left the programs
accounted for 95% of their programs,

00:28:51.183 --> 00:28:55.510
the programs accounted for 90% of
the TARP dollars that were in place and

00:28:55.510 --> 00:28:58.370
75% of the dollars were
already out the door.

00:28:58.370 --> 00:29:03.387
And the money we put into banks and
insurance companies all came back,

00:29:03.387 --> 00:29:05.775
plus just about $50 billion.

00:29:05.775 --> 00:29:11.153
And it prevented a disaster,
but you don't get credit for

00:29:11.153 --> 00:29:14.660
a disaster that people don't see.

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:18.350
When we went in and asked for the TARP,

00:29:21.530 --> 00:29:26.460
and painted the picture of
the economy was turning way down.

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:31.300
It was going to get much worse a number
of months out no matter what we did.

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:32.670
Barney Frank said to me,

00:29:32.670 --> 00:29:35.565
Barney Frank was chairman of the House
Financial Services Committee, he said,

00:29:35.565 --> 00:29:40.065
Hank this is going to be very difficult
because you can't prove a counterfactual.

00:29:40.065 --> 00:29:42.847
And that was at midnight, and
I said what's a counterfactual?

00:29:42.847 --> 00:29:45.158
And he said,
well you're not going to get credit for,

00:29:45.158 --> 00:29:48.675
you know people are going to be very angry
because they're going to be hurting, and

00:29:48.675 --> 00:29:51.715
you're not going to get credit for
saving a disaster they didn't see.

00:29:53.735 --> 00:29:58.720
But the biggest thing we did
with TARP that was unpopular,

00:29:59.990 --> 00:30:05.040
was people wanted to punish the banks, and

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:08.770
they wanted us to be tougher on the banks.

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:15.530
And we did something with TARP that
was never been done before, was

00:30:15.530 --> 00:30:20.800
extraordinarily innovative, and I don't
think people to this day understand it.

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:25.440
Which is when we switched,
which is another story, when

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:31.229
I went to congress and said we're not
going to put capital in the banks.

00:30:32.420 --> 00:30:34.390
We are going to buy a liquid assets.

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:40.970
And the reason I did that was, only time
I'd ever seen capital go in the banks,

00:30:40.970 --> 00:30:43.020
had been when they were nationalized.

00:30:43.020 --> 00:30:47.140
So bank is ready to fail, you nationalize
them, you're very tough on them, right?

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:47.762
That's what you do with.

00:30:47.762 --> 00:30:49.980
That's we did with Fannie and Freddie.

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:52.880
That's what the Europeans did,
you know, the British,

00:30:52.880 --> 00:30:54.390
they only put capital in two banks.

00:30:54.390 --> 00:30:57.280
They nationalized,
the Europeans, a handful.

00:30:57.280 --> 00:31:01.330
So I thought we needed to
recapitalize the system.

00:31:01.330 --> 00:31:04.060
And I had a theory about
buying liquid assets.

00:31:05.200 --> 00:31:08.960
But I told Barney Frank,
we need the authority to put capital in,

00:31:08.960 --> 00:31:11.100
because I thought we might
have to nationalize a few.

00:31:12.100 --> 00:31:14.710
So, when we got the, but

00:31:14.710 --> 00:31:20.270
the two weeks we were up in Congress,
the world seemed to change.

00:31:20.270 --> 00:31:26.920
Wachovia went down, Wamu went down,
the Europeans had to bail out their banks.

00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:29.438
So I went to George Bush, and

00:31:29.438 --> 00:31:34.970
I would say you're in a crisis,
you're dealing with big,

00:31:34.970 --> 00:31:37.370
ugly problems, there are not going to
be elegant, perfect solutions.

00:31:37.370 --> 00:31:41.370
So you gotta make decisions, and if you're
wrong, you gotta be willing to change.

00:31:41.370 --> 00:31:46.020
So I went and I said, guess what, it's not
going to work, buy your liquid assets.

00:31:46.020 --> 00:31:47.290
We have to put capital in banks.

00:31:47.290 --> 00:31:49.200
And he said, well, how are you going to
explain that to the whole world?

00:31:49.200 --> 00:31:50.940
I said, I don't know,
I'll just say, we made a mistake,

00:31:50.940 --> 00:31:52.060
we're going to put capital in the banks.

00:31:52.060 --> 00:31:57.460
And he said, good, do what you're
going to do to save the The economy.

00:31:57.460 --> 00:32:04.659
So, but then what we did was
we made attractive terms.

00:32:04.659 --> 00:32:08.388
Because we wanted to
recapitalize the banking system,

00:32:08.388 --> 00:32:10.780
and we put capital in 700 banks.

00:32:12.300 --> 00:32:20.110
So I chose stability over doing
what would be politically popular.

00:32:20.110 --> 00:32:21.870
&gt;&gt; And what about the misconceptions or

00:32:21.870 --> 00:32:27.630
the personal misconceptions when it came
to exaggerated bonuses, golden parachutes?

00:32:27.630 --> 00:32:31.256
Why not put something in place that
makes it very clear to the people that

00:32:31.256 --> 00:32:34.412
individuals within those
institutions are not benefiting?

00:32:34.412 --> 00:32:39.684
&gt;&gt; You're right, that of all the things,
well first of all, if we'd done that,

00:32:39.684 --> 00:32:46.200
if we had put all those conditions on, we
could have easily put those conditions on.

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:53.830
And we did more than that when you
look at AIG or Fannie and Freddie.

00:32:53.830 --> 00:32:57.710
But if I'd done that,
then every bank is the tallest midget.

00:32:57.710 --> 00:33:00.600
They don't need capital
until they're ready to fail.

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:03.700
And you just would have had
a number of serial failures.

00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:08.050
So what we did,
we put in a number of provisions, and

00:33:08.050 --> 00:33:14.190
we put in a provision on parachutes,
okay, but not bonuses.

00:33:14.190 --> 00:33:18.390
If we said we're going to determine how
you pay people, then many banks would say,

00:33:18.390 --> 00:33:19.730
well we're not going to fail.

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:22.010
But we recapitalized the banking system.

00:33:22.010 --> 00:33:26.793
So it was very unpopular, but
look at where we are relative to Europe.

00:33:26.793 --> 00:33:30.932
The governments are bailing out the banks
which is bailing out the government

00:33:30.932 --> 00:33:31.510
and so on.

00:33:31.510 --> 00:33:34.590
Our banks were recapitalized.

00:33:34.590 --> 00:33:39.760
And that put everything in
place that was needed for

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:43.490
the stress test and for
recapitalizing the banking system.

00:33:43.490 --> 00:33:44.470
But you're totally right.

00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:47.730
And of all the things that bothered me,
and

00:33:47.730 --> 00:33:54.080
I thought was egregious, I just thought it
was so beyond the pale, was those bonuses.

00:33:54.080 --> 00:34:01.270
Because after the fact, bankers didn't
have to say we've done anything wrong.

00:34:01.270 --> 00:34:04.410
They just have to say,
thank you to the United States of America.

00:34:05.620 --> 00:34:09.780
What was done saved the system,
even if our bank might not have gone down,

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:11.300
which they all might have said.

00:34:11.300 --> 00:34:16.950
And so we are going to show
some self awareness and

00:34:16.950 --> 00:34:20.686
sense of decency and so on.

00:34:20.686 --> 00:34:23.929
So I sat there dying watching the bonuses,
but

00:34:23.929 --> 00:34:29.099
there weren't requirements and
I would do the same thing all over again.

00:34:29.099 --> 00:34:32.246
I tell you that's why we recovered so
quickly and

00:34:32.246 --> 00:34:37.270
why it worked, and the capital came back,
but people are angry.

00:34:37.270 --> 00:34:38.130
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for that.

00:34:39.540 --> 00:34:41.120
You've been very accomplished.

00:34:41.120 --> 00:34:46.146
You've actually accomplished more than any
of us could wish to on several fronts.

00:34:46.146 --> 00:34:47.032
And after all of that,

00:34:47.032 --> 00:34:50.910
you decided to dedicate the rest of your
life to the work at the Paulson Institute.

00:34:50.910 --> 00:34:54.730
So what is it about the US-China
future collaboration that you place so

00:34:54.730 --> 00:34:58.280
much value on, and
are there any specific opportunities or

00:34:58.280 --> 00:35:00.880
sectors that you think we
should keep an eye on?

00:35:03.230 --> 00:35:09.835
&gt;&gt; Well, I would say this, first of all,
as I look around here at you all.

00:35:09.835 --> 00:35:14.355
Because you're here, we're all similar.

00:35:14.355 --> 00:35:17.280
We all want to make a difference, right?

00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:20.572
We all want to have a rich personal life.

00:35:20.572 --> 00:35:23.720
We want to do something professionally
where we make a difference.

00:35:24.990 --> 00:35:30.232
And my theory is always you need to say,
in today's world

00:35:30.232 --> 00:35:34.570
where there's so many talented people,
where do I have a comparative advantage?

00:35:35.630 --> 00:35:40.482
Where do I have the greatest chance
to make a difference where there's

00:35:40.482 --> 00:35:45.840
an intersection of something that I can
do well, and something that I enjoy?

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:51.233
Okay, so two things happened
over the course of my career.

00:35:51.233 --> 00:35:56.870
One of them was serendipitously,
I got to Asia.

00:35:56.870 --> 00:36:03.520
I got there at the right time,
I met the Chinese leaders and I was there.

00:36:03.520 --> 00:36:09.032
And I would just say if you're interested,
my Chinese version of my book came out.

00:36:09.032 --> 00:36:11.730
Dealing with China,
which is the book I wrote.

00:36:11.730 --> 00:36:15.990
I wrote the first one on the crisis
was On the Brink, Dealing with China.

00:36:15.990 --> 00:36:22.930
The first third of the book is
about investment banking in China

00:36:24.260 --> 00:36:29.303
bringing how capital markets transactions
brought western know-how and

00:36:29.303 --> 00:36:34.040
capital, that the leaders used to
open up the country to competition.

00:36:34.040 --> 00:36:35.740
And so I was there.

00:36:35.740 --> 00:36:37.150
It really revived my career.

00:36:37.150 --> 00:36:38.330
I would've left Goldman Sachs.

00:36:38.330 --> 00:36:42.800
I was interested in maybe
doing some other things, and

00:36:42.800 --> 00:36:48.730
I worked on the first big public
offering for China Mobile privatization,

00:36:48.730 --> 00:36:52.670
then PetroChina,
then the first bank deal, Bank of China.

00:36:52.670 --> 00:36:59.130
So I worked alongside of people that
if you were to name the people,

00:36:59.130 --> 00:37:04.093
the guy who runs the Central Bank,
the Minister of Finance Lou Jiwei,

00:37:04.093 --> 00:37:06.700
Zhou Xiaochuan, the Central Bank,
Wang Qishan who

00:37:06.700 --> 00:37:11.880
now runs a big interdisciplinary committee
and who's on the standing committee.

00:37:11.880 --> 00:37:14.723
I worked with these people.

00:37:14.723 --> 00:37:19.385
President Xi Jinping, when I set up
the strategic economic dialogue,

00:37:19.385 --> 00:37:24.359
which I did as treasury secretary with
China, I've actually visited him in

00:37:24.359 --> 00:37:28.731
Hangzhou before I went to see the others,
the leaders in Beijing.

00:37:28.731 --> 00:37:35.484
And so I helped him plan, when Xi Jinping
visited the West Coast last September,

00:37:35.484 --> 00:37:41.860
he asked me to get together a group of
CEOs to moderate a discussion with him.

00:37:41.860 --> 00:37:45.850
So I know these people, so
I said I can make a difference there.

00:37:45.850 --> 00:37:47.850
And I always cared about conservation.

00:37:47.850 --> 00:37:52.580
Jim Morgan and
I worked on the Asia-Pacific Council of

00:37:52.580 --> 00:37:59.090
the Nature Conservancy, so my wife got
me involved in the Nature Conservancy.

00:37:59.090 --> 00:38:03.930
I worked in saving parks,
setting up parks in Hunan in China.

00:38:03.930 --> 00:38:06.250
So I've always cared about conservation.

00:38:06.250 --> 00:38:07.233
I've cared about the environment.

00:38:07.233 --> 00:38:12.447
And so if you care about the global
ecosystem, if you think climate

00:38:12.447 --> 00:38:17.380
change is the biggest economic
risk by far we face in the world,

00:38:17.380 --> 00:38:21.335
and you want to do something about it,
guess what?

00:38:21.335 --> 00:38:22.947
China's the place to be,

00:38:22.947 --> 00:38:27.871
because if they don't make progress
nothing else we do makes much difference.

00:38:27.871 --> 00:38:34.700
And so I basically said, I care about the
environment, I care about conservation.

00:38:34.700 --> 00:38:35.714
I've made enough money.

00:38:35.714 --> 00:38:37.640
I don't need to make more money,
I'm going to do an NGO.

00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:40.002
So I set up the Paulson Institute.

00:38:40.002 --> 00:38:44.840
And also, the Nature Conservancy asked me
to do for them what I've done in China,

00:38:44.840 --> 00:38:50.110
which I set up the Latin American Council
of the Nature Conservancy.

00:38:50.110 --> 00:38:54.326
So I got back from China on Saturday and
on the 9th, I'm going to Oaxaca,

00:38:54.326 --> 00:38:58.570
Mexico where we're going to have our
meeting with the Nature Conservancy.

00:38:58.570 --> 00:39:03.652
And so
I work with business because in China,

00:39:03.652 --> 00:39:10.491
I have a US-China CEO consul
on sustainable urbanization.

00:39:10.491 --> 00:39:14.631
So I go there with people like Tim Cook
from Apple and Doug MacMillon from

00:39:14.631 --> 00:39:18.909
Walmart and Ginny Rometty from IBM and
Mary Barrow from General Motors and

00:39:18.909 --> 00:39:23.188
we work with Jack Ma, Alibaba,
the State Grid construction companies.

00:39:23.188 --> 00:39:28.160
So we're doing the things that I
know how to do and I like to do.

00:39:28.160 --> 00:39:35.105
And I must like work, because I'm no
longer on a private plane going there,

00:39:35.105 --> 00:39:39.537
I'm on United Airlines, and-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:39:39.537 --> 00:39:41.488
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for that.

00:39:41.488 --> 00:39:42.292
&gt;&gt; And I mean I can do this,

00:39:42.292 --> 00:39:44.594
I'd love to do this for hours but
we're unfortunately out of time.

00:39:44.594 --> 00:39:48.070
So, one final question
before we move to Q&amp;A.

00:39:48.070 --> 00:39:51.230
You and Wendy have been married for
just under 50 years.

00:39:51.230 --> 00:39:54.080
Myself and Donna, who's here with
us today, have been married for

00:39:54.080 --> 00:39:55.085
just under two months.

00:39:55.085 --> 00:39:57.173
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:39:57.173 --> 00:39:58.465
[LAUGH] do you have any advice for us?

00:39:58.465 --> 00:40:00.911
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:40:00.911 --> 00:40:06.580
I would say people always make jokes and
so on about this, but to me it's serious.

00:40:06.580 --> 00:40:09.779
And I think the key thing is,

00:40:09.779 --> 00:40:14.711
[COUGH] we got married when we were young,
and

00:40:14.711 --> 00:40:20.810
we grew together, and
we kept common interest.

00:40:20.810 --> 00:40:25.180
I've seen so many couples,
they get married and they grow apart.

00:40:27.010 --> 00:40:31.410
And so we've done all of our
conservation work together.

00:40:32.640 --> 00:40:37.801
We've done so much jointly,
she's helped me immensely in China.

00:40:37.801 --> 00:40:43.410
Many of our most important relationships
were ones that we both built.

00:40:43.410 --> 00:40:48.071
We were in, last Thursday,
it's the two of us,

00:40:48.071 --> 00:40:52.300
I was asking her,
please stay an extra day.

00:40:52.300 --> 00:40:56.415
Because we're going to have dinner
with Premiere Li Keqiang and his wife,

00:40:56.415 --> 00:40:59.190
Cheng Hong, and
she speaks beautiful English.

00:40:59.190 --> 00:41:04.620
She studied American nature writing,

00:41:04.620 --> 00:41:09.570
literature study, the transcendentalists,
Thoreau and Emerson.

00:41:09.570 --> 00:41:15.568
And so Wendy was there,
we had a workshop on wetlands

00:41:15.568 --> 00:41:20.490
conservation, and
Wendy was there doing that with me.

00:41:20.490 --> 00:41:29.510
So I think that the key is, you need to
find things that you can do together.

00:41:29.510 --> 00:41:32.140
Everybody talks about me as
being a bird watcher, okay?

00:41:32.140 --> 00:41:36.485
I like being outdoors, I really don't
like bird watching as much as Wendy does.

00:41:36.485 --> 00:41:39.586
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I do it because I've got bad eyes,

00:41:39.586 --> 00:41:43.839
I don't see them as well, but
I do it because I enjoy it and

00:41:43.839 --> 00:41:47.759
I'm doing it with her, and
so we find those things.

00:41:47.759 --> 00:41:53.371
And then the other thing I really
find that is terribly important is,

00:41:53.371 --> 00:41:58.208
when there are differences,
to talk about them and learn,

00:41:58.208 --> 00:42:03.045
the most important thing you
can learn is to say I'm sorry,

00:42:03.045 --> 00:42:07.046
even if you think its
the other person's fault.

00:42:07.046 --> 00:42:11.209
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; If you learn to say you’re sorry and

00:42:11.209 --> 00:42:15.430
just really learn to say you’re sorry.

00:42:15.430 --> 00:42:18.900
So, we would always, every week,

00:42:18.900 --> 00:42:22.960
no matter what,
no matter how busy I was at Treasury or

00:42:22.960 --> 00:42:27.890
whatever, we would go for
a long walk or a bike ride.

00:42:27.890 --> 00:42:32.158
And she tells the story of the time when
we were going for a bike ride at Treasury,

00:42:32.158 --> 00:42:34.465
when I was so, when my mind was elsewhere.

00:42:34.465 --> 00:42:40.103
I rode the bike right into a gate and
collapsed on the pavement, so.

00:42:40.103 --> 00:42:45.170
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But these relationships you get out of,

00:42:45.170 --> 00:42:48.460
I will just say this to you.

00:42:48.460 --> 00:42:53.505
There is nothing, there's not a perfect
job, there's nothing that's perfect.

00:42:53.505 --> 00:42:57.260
There's, I've got as close to perfect
wife as you can have but nothing,

00:42:57.260 --> 00:43:00.010
no marriage,
anything is going to be perfect.

00:43:00.010 --> 00:43:05.250
You get out of it what you put into it,
and so you need to keep investing.

00:43:05.250 --> 00:43:10.119
And if you don't continue to invest in
your marriage, no matter how much you're

00:43:10.119 --> 00:43:14.213
in love now 10, 15, or
20 years from now you'll grow apart.

00:43:14.213 --> 00:43:21.462
So you just, it's just like anything else,
if you value it you will keep it.

00:43:21.462 --> 00:43:29.240
&gt;&gt; Well I'm sorry, and
[LAUGH] we'll move on to Q and A now.

00:43:29.240 --> 00:43:33.160
So when you're asking the question
please stand up and state your name.

00:43:34.890 --> 00:43:39.138
&gt;&gt; I wanted to ask what do you think
of the financial technology sector?

00:43:39.138 --> 00:43:41.251
And how do you think
that's going to evolve and

00:43:41.251 --> 00:43:43.944
change the relationship between banks and
consumers?

00:43:48.220 --> 00:43:52.456
&gt;&gt; What do you think of the evolution
of the financial technology center?

00:43:52.456 --> 00:43:57.766
And how do you think this will effect
the relationship between the banks and

00:43:57.766 --> 00:43:58.870
the consumer?

00:43:58.870 --> 00:43:59.909
&gt;&gt; The evolution of what?

00:43:59.909 --> 00:44:02.203
&gt;&gt; The financial technology sector.

00:44:02.203 --> 00:44:05.214
&gt;&gt; The financial technology sector,
Fintech okay?

00:44:05.214 --> 00:44:05.727
&gt;&gt; Fintech.

00:44:05.727 --> 00:44:10.228
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Sorry about that.

00:44:10.228 --> 00:44:13.298
&gt;&gt; Okay, because I tell you,

00:44:13.298 --> 00:44:18.370
it's amazing what's going on and the time,

00:44:18.370 --> 00:44:26.390
and you look at what's going on in China,
with Alipay and so on.

00:44:26.390 --> 00:44:28.680
So it's extraordinary, and

00:44:28.680 --> 00:44:34.080
it is in China in particular,
where you don't

00:44:34.080 --> 00:44:39.610
have all the financial infrastructure
that we've got in other countries.

00:44:39.610 --> 00:44:44.020
It is growing, very, very quickly,
I think it's exciting.

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:50.970
I think a paperless, if we ever get
to a paperless financial system,

00:44:54.230 --> 00:44:58.640
it will make a big difference
in terms of transparency and

00:44:58.640 --> 00:45:01.680
all kinds of other things
in terms of taxes.

00:45:03.150 --> 00:45:08.740
And I think the thing we
need to be careful about is,

00:45:08.740 --> 00:45:14.630
I learned and one of the lessons
from the financial crisis is,

00:45:14.630 --> 00:45:19.470
you can sometimes have too
much complexity or innovation.

00:45:19.470 --> 00:45:23.584
And one of the things that happened
before the financial crisis,

00:45:23.584 --> 00:45:26.890
is our financial system
outgrew our regulatory system.

00:45:27.990 --> 00:45:34.810
And so I do worry about regulation,

00:45:35.900 --> 00:45:41.869
transparency in places, and
I watch the money market funds.

00:45:42.980 --> 00:45:48.688
The scariest thing for me, and
the closest I could see to the financial

00:45:48.688 --> 00:45:54.881
crisis bleeding over from Wall Street to
Main Street was when we had three and

00:45:54.881 --> 00:46:00.719
a half or four trillion dollars worth
of money market funds imploding.

00:46:00.719 --> 00:46:03.714
And they're the ones that were
funding the commercial paper.

00:46:03.714 --> 00:46:08.654
So I was hearing from AAA companies saying
we can't sell our commercial paper,

00:46:08.654 --> 00:46:13.630
and we're not going to be able to even
make our quarterly dividend payment.

00:46:13.630 --> 00:46:18.524
And I could realize if they start paying
their suppliers, then it goes, it just

00:46:18.524 --> 00:46:23.947
goes, pretty soon it hits small industrial
companies and the whole system collapses.

00:46:23.947 --> 00:46:28.752
And so I think to make sure that this,

00:46:28.752 --> 00:46:33.402
what I call fintech doesn't grow so

00:46:33.402 --> 00:46:38.210
quickly, that it's unregulated.

00:46:38.210 --> 00:46:42.885
And we needed to make sure that
there's the right protections and

00:46:42.885 --> 00:46:45.770
the right liquidity, and so on.

00:46:45.770 --> 00:46:50.400
&gt;&gt; So if there's an app that controls your
entire portfolio at the click of a button,

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:53.763
how would that affect
Goldman's Private Wealth Management Team?

00:46:53.763 --> 00:46:59.169
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I'll tell you, for

00:46:59.169 --> 00:47:04.574
a long time, I remember,

00:47:04.574 --> 00:47:09.254
and this'll date me.

00:47:09.254 --> 00:47:14.407
So you all were in primary school,

00:47:14.407 --> 00:47:22.049
but when we had the telecom
internet bubble burst,

00:47:22.049 --> 00:47:28.996
when I was at Goldman Sachs in about 2000.

00:47:28.996 --> 00:47:33.396
And the years running up to that
everybody was saying, well,

00:47:33.396 --> 00:47:37.300
how's the internet going to
change our business model?

00:47:37.300 --> 00:47:39.941
How's it going to
disintermediate what we do?

00:47:39.941 --> 00:47:44.838
And I remember then we tried
to set up a technology

00:47:44.838 --> 00:47:49.420
platform for a high net worth force.

00:47:49.420 --> 00:47:52.800
And I continue to believe that

00:47:54.130 --> 00:47:59.400
there are going to be a good number
of people that are going to want

00:47:59.400 --> 00:48:04.120
real Advice and real judgment and
customized, but there's

00:48:04.120 --> 00:48:09.285
a lot that needs to be done and can be
done more efficiently through technology.

00:48:09.285 --> 00:48:15.370
So, but that's the story,
the concern and what you

00:48:15.370 --> 00:48:20.090
all are going to deal with, we're dealing
with right now, is going to be a big,

00:48:20.090 --> 00:48:25.470
big factor in your business lifetimes
is the dark side of technology.

00:48:25.470 --> 00:48:29.600
It makes all of our lives better, we're
not going to turn back the clock, but

00:48:29.600 --> 00:48:33.610
I will tell you, when you look
at automation and technology and

00:48:33.610 --> 00:48:37.660
what's doing the labor markets, and
the number of people that are going to be.

00:48:39.330 --> 00:48:42.448
Anything that can be automated or
routinized, will be.

00:48:42.448 --> 00:48:43.680
So that's,

00:48:43.680 --> 00:48:47.820
what you're going to see is people at
the top are going to do very well.

00:48:47.820 --> 00:48:50.754
And there's going to be a lot
of people without jobs or

00:48:50.754 --> 00:48:52.400
without the jobs they want.

00:48:52.400 --> 00:48:58.455
And how we come up with a political system
that lets our democracy work and lets our

00:48:58.455 --> 00:49:04.174
economy continue to flourish, is going to
take a lot of interesting thought.

00:49:04.174 --> 00:49:06.225
And there's nothing you
need to be afraid of but

00:49:06.225 --> 00:49:08.850
that's another reason why it
comes back to my advice to you.

00:49:08.850 --> 00:49:12.350
You can afford anything other than not,
you need to keep learning and

00:49:12.350 --> 00:49:16.690
growing in any job you get and
get something that plays to your skill.

00:49:16.690 --> 00:49:21.688
It's going to take judgement and
take wisdom and they can't be,

00:49:21.688 --> 00:49:26.280
you're not going to be
disintermediated by technology.

00:49:26.280 --> 00:49:29.885
&gt;&gt; I think we have time for
one more question.

00:49:33.879 --> 00:49:36.660
&gt;&gt; Peter Stradling, thank you for
coming to talk to us.

00:49:36.660 --> 00:49:40.980
So I was just thinking about, given
the immediate reporting on the crisis in

00:49:40.980 --> 00:49:45.830
the book Too Big to Fail and the narrative
that both those things cultivated.

00:49:45.830 --> 00:49:49.298
Is there anything about this prevailing
narrative that you feel is fundamentally

00:49:49.298 --> 00:49:51.521
misunderstood or
you would want the public to know?

00:49:51.521 --> 00:49:55.145
You mentioned TARP and it's
implementation but are there other things?

00:49:55.145 --> 00:50:01.560
&gt;&gt; I would say that I think Too Big
to Fail got it largely right.

00:50:01.560 --> 00:50:06.350
If you want to read the narrative that got
it totally right, you'd read On the Brink,

00:50:06.350 --> 00:50:12.520
because that was a first hand account,

00:50:12.520 --> 00:50:21.290
and no one has been able to quarrel with
any fact in the book the way it's written.

00:50:21.290 --> 00:50:26.925
But I would say that the other thing
that people ask about all the time.

00:50:26.925 --> 00:50:32.730
When they say if you made major mistakes
and I say, well I think the major ones, we

00:50:32.730 --> 00:50:36.830
made a lot of mistakes, but the major ones
we corrected right on the battlefield.

00:50:36.830 --> 00:50:42.140
So, putting, reversing ourselves, putting
capital in the banks, or with Fanny or

00:50:42.140 --> 00:50:46.360
Freddie, I remember I said, if we had
unlimited authorities or unspecified

00:50:46.360 --> 00:50:51.750
I wouldn't have to take the bazooka out,
and I took it out when I had to.

00:50:51.750 --> 00:50:56.910
But, I would say, we still have
a surprising number of people,

00:50:56.910 --> 00:51:00.970
that despite the fact that Bernanke,
Geithner, and I are all generally regarded

00:51:00.970 --> 00:51:04.590
to be straight shooters, don't believe
us when they say, we didn't have any

00:51:04.590 --> 00:51:08.540
authority, legal authority,
that would have worked to save Lehman.

00:51:08.540 --> 00:51:12.130
And the fact is, but no one can tell
us what that authority was we had.

00:51:12.130 --> 00:51:17.650
Because, we couldn't,
we had no authority to,

00:51:17.650 --> 00:51:20.810
guaranteed debt, or to put in capital.

00:51:21.830 --> 00:51:25.890
And, what we learned when
Bear Stearns was going down,

00:51:25.890 --> 00:51:32.280
was a fed loan didn't work when
a bank was, frankly, unravelling.

00:51:32.280 --> 00:51:36.410
Everybody pulls their money and run,
and so we were fortunate enough to

00:51:36.410 --> 00:51:41.000
have a buyer there, JP Morgan and
it guaranteed the trading book.

00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:43.860
And so when Lehman went,
we had AIG, Lehman,

00:51:43.860 --> 00:51:45.639
and Merrill all going at the same time.

00:51:46.980 --> 00:51:53.140
And frankly, it was terrible but we got
the best possible outcome because if

00:51:53.140 --> 00:51:58.930
Lehman had been bought, then they
wouldn't have been anyone to buy Merrill.

00:51:58.930 --> 00:52:02.180
And Merrill was bigger and
they were going down immediately.

00:52:02.180 --> 00:52:07.660
And with AIG,
we lucked out because the perception

00:52:07.660 --> 00:52:12.130
with all us was there's this big
liquidity crisis at the holding company.

00:52:12.130 --> 00:52:17.050
Which was like a big hedge fund and the
underlying insurance companies which had

00:52:17.050 --> 00:52:20.080
individual credit ratings were money good.

00:52:20.080 --> 00:52:25.350
So the treasury could loan against
those and the market accepted it.

00:52:25.350 --> 00:52:27.660
But by the time the market figured out,
guess what,

00:52:27.660 --> 00:52:32.150
that wasn't the case, we now had
the tarp and we could put capital in.

00:52:32.150 --> 00:52:37.788
But at the end of the day, I realized

00:52:37.788 --> 00:52:43.410
that I've given up trying to explain
that because unless you were close to it

00:52:43.410 --> 00:52:47.830
you're not going to really
understand it that well anyway.

00:52:47.830 --> 00:52:54.604
And the fact is, bailouts shouldn't be,
the whole thing was unpopular,

00:52:54.604 --> 00:53:01.180
and I don't think bailouts should
ever be popular in the United States.

00:53:02.600 --> 00:53:07.100
And when people screw up,
if they take a risk and

00:53:07.100 --> 00:53:10.562
they succeed they should be rewarded and
if they fail they should fail.

00:53:10.562 --> 00:53:16.300
But the financial system is such that
if it goes under everybody pays.

00:53:16.300 --> 00:53:21.030
The only other thing, which I think
I wish people know and understood and

00:53:21.030 --> 00:53:24.823
I think this is quite important.

00:53:24.823 --> 00:53:30.780
Is that when there's a financial crisis
everybody always blames the banks.

00:53:30.780 --> 00:53:34.570
&gt;From the beginning of times
banks get 100% of the blame.

00:53:34.570 --> 00:53:39.750
And banks make all kinds of mistakes and
so some good comes out of that.

00:53:39.750 --> 00:53:44.400
But the root cause of a financial
crisis is never the banks.

00:53:44.400 --> 00:53:49.500
Don't let anybody tell you it is,
it is flawed government policies, and

00:53:49.500 --> 00:53:52.890
I don't care what the political system is.

00:53:52.890 --> 00:53:54.970
Whenever you've got a big economy and

00:53:54.970 --> 00:53:59.130
you've got a banking system,
there'll be flawed government policies.

00:53:59.130 --> 00:54:03.935
They lead to excesses, excesses manifest
themselves in the banking system and

00:54:03.935 --> 00:54:06.060
there'll be failures.

00:54:06.060 --> 00:54:08.027
So the best you can hope for

00:54:08.027 --> 00:54:13.937
is to get to it before the excesses become
too great and have the tools you need.

00:54:13.937 --> 00:54:16.570
And so the Fintech question,

00:54:16.570 --> 00:54:21.836
the thing I'd be asking myself
all the time about Fintech,

00:54:21.836 --> 00:54:25.851
is everybody now is
focused on the big banks.

00:54:25.851 --> 00:54:30.390
But I think the problem is that
if you regulate them so much and

00:54:30.390 --> 00:54:35.560
turn them into utilities you
force the risk out elsewhere.

00:54:35.560 --> 00:54:39.870
And a lot of the risk was in
the tri-party repo system,

00:54:39.870 --> 00:54:43.330
was in the shadow banking market,
money markets and so on.

00:54:43.330 --> 00:54:49.680
And the basic risk comes in our financial
system because whatever the size is,

00:54:49.680 --> 00:54:56.080
I forgot what number is, 13, $14 trillion
or whatever, 60% of that is deposits.

00:54:56.080 --> 00:55:02.900
Okay, the rest is wholesale funding and
so you're always going to have risk.

00:55:02.900 --> 00:55:09.590
And so the problem is if you
just only blame the banks

00:55:09.590 --> 00:55:13.920
you get to this,
it's going really far in the US, but

00:55:13.920 --> 00:55:18.220
then you don't focus on
cleaning up the basic problems.

00:55:18.220 --> 00:55:23.010
What leads us to borrow too much,
save too little, okay,

00:55:23.010 --> 00:55:25.240
why don't we get around to fixing Fanny or
Freddy?

00:55:26.280 --> 00:55:31.770
If we have a vocal rule and
banks can't do market making and

00:55:31.770 --> 00:55:37.020
you got liquidity problems and you've got
interest rates that are almost zero and

00:55:37.020 --> 00:55:39.750
money is seeking out yield.

00:55:39.750 --> 00:55:43.900
You're going to have bubbles and what
about the bond funds and liquidity there?

00:55:43.900 --> 00:55:48.488
There's so again I'd say don't
just focus on the banks and

00:55:48.488 --> 00:55:54.088
don't ever believe that the banks
are the only problem because that'll

00:55:54.088 --> 00:56:00.010
just cause you to beat up on the banks and
we won't deal with the root causes.

00:56:00.010 --> 00:56:01.750
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for
your time Mr Secretary.

00:56:01.750 --> 00:56:02.762
&gt;&gt; Okay, thank you, thank you.

00:56:02.762 --> 00:56:05.353
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:56:05.353 --> 00:56:11.560
[MUSIC]

